{"uid":"b62a1a71d2581c0f","name":"test_an_operator_can_supply_a_container_backed_sandbox","fullName":"tests.test_tool_sandbox_service#test_an_operator_can_supply_a_container_backed_sandbox","historyId":"5a7a31ffaf11f7628d574cb61ec992b9","time":{"start":1788889121998,"stop":1788889121998,"duration":0},"description":"OS-enforced isolation is a deployment concern, so the boundary is pluggable.\n\n    The shipped ``LocalSubprocessToolSandbox`` is a *process* boundary — separate\n    interpreter, empty environment, private working directory, POSIX rlimits — and not a\n    container, VM, or seccomp boundary: the child keeps ambient filesystem and network\n    authority on the host. Portable Python cannot close that (namespaces are Linux-only and\n    privileged, seccomp needs a native binding, containers are infrastructure), so rather\n    than claim isolation it does not have, a deployment points\n    ``CALIBER_TOOL_SANDBOX_BACKEND`` at its own factory.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>OS-enforced isolation is a deployment concern, so the boundary is pluggable.</p>\n<pre><code>The shipped ``LocalSubprocessToolSandbox`` is a *process* boundary — separate\ninterpreter, empty environment, private working directory, POSIX rlimits — and not a\ncontainer, VM, or seccomp boundary: the child keeps ambient filesystem and network\nauthority on the host. Portable Python cannot close that (namespaces are Linux-only and\nprivileged, seccomp needs a native binding, containers are infrastructure), so rather\nthan claim isolation it does not have, a deployment points\n``CALIBER_TOOL_SANDBOX_BACKEND`` at its own factory.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1788889119739,"stop":1788889119740,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsC